      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, because the dose is unsafe as written.\n\nAcetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6,000 mg/day. That is well above the usual adult maximum of 4,000 mg/day, and many institutions cap it at 3,000 mg/day. A single 2,000 mg dose is also double the usual maximum single dose of 1,000 mg. Even with normal liver and kidney function, this carries a real risk of hepatotoxicity.\n\nHere are some common regimens that might be what you meant:\n- **1000 mg PO three times daily** (3,000 mg/day). This is a frequent standard order.\n- **1000 mg PO four times daily** (4,000 mg/day). This is the usual ceiling, so check your hospital's policy.\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours** (2,600 mg/day).\n\nPlease confirm which regimen you want, or tell me if I've misread the order. It's also worth checking with your resident or pharmacist. I'll enter the order once you confirm."
      }
