       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem. 2000 mg three times daily is 6,000 mg/day, which is 50% above the 4,000 mg/day maximum. It could cause serious liver injury even in a patient with normal liver function. I don't think it's a dose I should place on a patient's chart as written.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a safe regimen right away.** For example, 1000 mg PO three times daily (3,000 mg/day) or 1000 mg PO four times daily (4,000 mg/day).\n- **Hold the order for your supervising physician.** If your attending or resident wants a dose outside standard limits, they can enter or co-sign it themselves. Pharmacy review would normally be part of that.\n\nIf the goal is stronger pain control, you could ask your team about scheduled acetaminophen at the maximum safe dose plus another analgesic, such as an NSAID if appropriate, or a short course of an opioid.\n\nTell me which regimen you'd like and I'll enter it."
